LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — Why count: the dialogue", "13", "<p>Three modules built a ledger worth believing. This module tests the belief: the <b>count</b> — the moment the ledger's testimony and the shelf's reality are held against each other, and the differences are made to speak.</p><p><b>The count is a dialogue, not a verdict.</b> The ledger says 96; the shelf says 93. Neither is automatically right: the ledger may have missed a movement (the transfer receipt never entered), the shelf may be hiding stock (the mis-shelved cartons two aisles over), or three units may genuinely be gone. The count's product is not a corrected number — it is the QUESTION, held open long enough to be answered (Chapter 6's investigation discipline), and only then the adjustment, in the open, with its reason. A firm that treats counting as typing shelf numbers over ledger numbers has built an eraser, not a control — and Chapter 9 convicts that eraser by name.</p><p><b>What counting confirms — and what it cannot fix.</b> Where modules 1-3 were kept (movements documented, gates honest, identity captured), the count is a CONFIRMATION: small differences, explainable, adjusted with confidence. Where they were not, the count is a CONFESSION — and the critical understanding is that counting cannot substitute for the disciplines it tests: the firm that counts weekly because its movements are chaos is bailing a boat it refuses to patch; every count resets the water level and the leak remains. Counting frequency is not a virtue by itself; the virtue is a ledger that counting keeps AGREEING with, which is earned upstream.</p><p><b>The three jobs of a counting regime.</b> <b>Accuracy</b>: the operational numbers everyone trades on (the four numbers of every bin) verified on a cadence proportionate to their importance. <b>Accountability</b>: the custodian's number (module 1) made fair — the storekeeper answerable for ₦11m deserves the periodic, documented moment where their custody is confirmed, which protects the honest custodian exactly as much as it exposes the other kind. <b>Deterrence</b>: the counted warehouse steals from itself less — not because counts catch everything, but because the KNOWLEDGE of counting changes behaviour; the cadence's very existence is a control, and irregular, unpredictable elements in the programme (Chapter 5) sharpen it.</p><p><b>The count's own economics.</b> Counting costs hours and interrupts trade, which is why this module spends two chapters on making it cheap: the full count made rare by the cycle programme, the cycle programme weighted by value so effort lands where money sits, and the freeze disciplines that keep a count from being a moving target counted twice. The end state — the well-run firm's relationship with counting — is undramatic by design: small, scheduled, boring counts whose variances are small, explained, and trending nowhere. Boring is the certificate.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Stock Reconciliation document", "13", "<p>The count's instrument is the <b>Stock Reconciliation</b> — the document that records what was counted and writes the ledger entries that make the books agree with the shelves. Its anatomy, and the weight each field carries.</p><ul><li><b>Purpose</b> — the document serves two distinct jobs under one roof: <b>Opening Stock</b> (Chapter 3's set piece — loading a warehouse's starting truth at go-live) and the ongoing <b>Stock Reconciliation</b> (the count adjustment). Same machinery, very different governance, and the purpose field says which is happening.</li><li><b>Posting date and time</b> — WHEN the count's truth applies. The chronology law at its most sensitive: the reconciliation asserts the counted quantity AS OF this moment, and every ledger read after it builds on that assertion — which is why the count's cutoff discipline (Chapter 4) and the posting timestamp must agree, and why backdated reconciliations are accountant-supervised territory (module 5 walks the valuation ripple they cause).</li><li><b>The rows</b> — item, warehouse, <b>counted quantity</b>, and (where the document is also asserting value — opening stock, or a deliberate revaluation) the <b>valuation rate</b>. Submission computes the difference against the ledger's current quantity and writes the adjusting entries: ins for surpluses, outs for shortfalls, valued per the item's costing. The adjustment IS a ledger movement like any other — appended, attributed, dated — which is the append-only law keeping even corrections honest.</li><li><b>Identity rows</b> — for batch-tracked items the count is per BATCH (the counted quantity of B-1147 at Ibadan, not just PARA-500 at Ibadan — the integrity arithmetic of module 3 depends on counts feeding it at batch level); for serialized items the reconciliation names serials (which units exist here — the serial audit's formal instrument). A reconciliation that counts identity-tracked items identity-blind repairs the quantity while corrupting the grid beneath it — half a fix that module 3's audits will re-flag.</li><li><b>Reason and reference</b> — the count sheet, the variance investigation's outcome, the reason CODE (Chapter 7's aggregation lives on these): the adjustment without a stated reason is the eraser wearing a document's clothes.</li></ul><p><b>What submission does — and its blast radius.</b> The adjusting entries change actual quantities, therefore projected quantities, therefore everything that reads them: availability promises, reorder triggers, valuations. A large reconciliation is a large event — reviewed before submission (the two-eyes threshold, by value of adjustment, exactly as write-offs and Material Receipts earned), and timed deliberately (the mid-trading-day reconciliation of a busy warehouse fights its own moving target; Chapter 4's windows exist for this). Draft, verify against the signed count records, check identity rows, check the rate columns are doing what you intend — the rate column on an ordinary count adjustment should usually assert nothing (quantity correction at existing valuation), and a rate typed carelessly there is a revaluation nobody meant to perform. Then submit, and the dialogue's answer becomes the ledger's next line.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Opening stock done right: the at-cost law", "15", "<p>Every implementation has one moment where years of a client's stock truth are keyed into a new system in a week — and one mistake at that moment poisons every number the system will ever produce. This chapter is the set piece: <b>go-live opening stock</b>, and the conviction that headlines it.</p><p><b>The sequence.</b> Opening stock is loaded by Stock Reconciliation (purpose: Opening Stock), warehouse by warehouse, and the sequence is not negotiable: <b>count first, then load</b> — the physical count of what actually exists at cutover, not the old system's report (the old system's errors are precisely what the fresh start should shed, and loading its numbers uncounted imports every ghost it accumulated), and not memory. The count runs with Chapter 4's disciplines (teams, sheets, verification) because it IS a full count — the most important one the firm will ever do, since every future count dialogues against the truth established here.</p><p><b>THE AT-COST LAW.</b> Opening stock enters at <b>cost — the item's honest acquisition cost — never at selling price</b>. This error is common enough, and catastrophic enough, to deserve its own wall: the client's team, keying under deadline, reaches for the price everyone knows (the shelf price, the price list) instead of the cost fewer people know — and the consequences cascade: the stock ASSET inflates by the entire embedded margin (a ₦30m inventory at cost booked as ₦42m at retail is a ₦12m fiction on the balance sheet); COGS inflates the moment selling begins (goods costed at selling price produce ZERO margin on paper — the firm sells briskly for months while reports insist it earns nothing); the distortion cannot be waved away, because valuation flows entry by entry (module 5) and every sale, transfer, and report inherits the poisoned rates until a supervised revaluation exercise — weeks of accountant-led correction — cleans what one week of care would have prevented. When a go-live's first-quarter margins look inexplicably terrible, this is the FIRST suspect, and the consultant who checks the opening rates against purchase records in hour one has usually found it.</p><p><b>Sourcing honest costs.</b> The rate column per item, filled from evidence: recent purchase invoices (the best source — last honest buy, landed where imports apply), the supplier's current lists where purchases are stale, and for the tail with no clean evidence, a defensible estimate DOCUMENTED as such with the accountant's sign-off. The discipline is proportionate: the A-value items (where the money sits) get invoice-level sourcing; the long tail gets a sensible method applied consistently. What no item gets: the selling price, zero (the opposite distortion — module 2's conviction), or a guess nobody wrote down.</p><p><b>Identity at opening.</b> Batch-tracked stock opens per batch WITH expiries — the counting team reads cartons, not just counts them, because an opening that loads PARA-500 as 400 units batch-blind has destroyed FEFO, the horizon, and the recall for every unit of that opening stock forever (the provenance cannot be invented later — module 3's retrofit warning at its sharpest). Serialized stock opens serial by serial from the physical units. Yes, this makes opening slower; it is the identity architecture's admission price, paid once, and the alternative is an identity system that begins life already broken.</p><p><b>The opening file.</b> Count sheets signed, rate sources referenced, the accountant's sign-off on method, the reconciliation documents themselves: the go-live's evidence bundle, filed. The balance sheet's stock figure was BORN here, and the auditor's first question at year one — <i>how was opening stock established?</i> — is answered from this file in minutes or from archaeology in weeks.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The full count: freeze, count, verify", "13", "<p>The wall-to-wall count — annual for most firms, plus the opening set piece — is a production, and productions succeed on planning. The run-book:</p><p><b>Plan.</b> Date chosen against trade (the quiet season, the weekend, the overnight); scope declared (which warehouses, all items); teams assigned in PAIRS with areas mapped (no aisle counted by its own daily custodian ALONE — the second pair of eyes is the accountability design, not an insult); count sheets or devices prepared — and the sheets are <b>blind</b>: the counter records what they SEE, without the ledger's expectation printed beside it, because a sheet showing expected 96 harvests 96 (the anchoring is human and universal; blindness is the cheap cure and Chapter 9's count-theatre conviction is its violation).</p><p><b>Freeze.</b> The count's enemy is movement: stock counted at 10:00 and moved at 10:30 is counted wrong somewhere. The clean solution is the <b>freeze window</b> — no movements in the counted warehouse during the count: receiving paused (trucks scheduled around it), issues and transfers held, sales stopped or (where trade cannot stop) strictly cut over: a hard timestamp, everything before it posted, everything after it segregated and excluded — physically set aside, documented, counted into the NEXT period's truth. The freeze is announced, short, and absolute; a leaky freeze produces variances that are artifacts of the leak, and the investigation chapter's first question about any surprising variance is always <i>did the freeze hold?</i></p><p><b>Count.</b> Pairs count their areas: physical units, honest UOMs (the carton-and-loose-units discipline — 47 cartons and 6 singles recorded as exactly that, the conversion arithmetic done by the system not the counter's head), identity read where tracked (batches from printed lots WITH quantities per lot; serials sighted). Completed sheets signed by the pair and surrendered — not corrected in the aisle, not reconciled on the fly.</p><p><b>Recount and verify.</b> Entries keyed (or synced) against the ledger; the variance list emerges; and the discipline that separates professionals: <b>material variances are RECOUNTED before they are believed</b> — a second, different pair to the same location, because the largest single source of count variances is the count itself (the missed shelf, the double-counted pallet, the UOM slip). Only variances that survive recount graduate to Chapter 6's investigation. Thresholds make this proportionate: kobo-level noise adjusts without ceremony; value and percentage lines route to recount; the biggest items get counted twice by design regardless.</p><p><b>Close.</b> The reconciliation document(s) drafted from verified counts, reviewed against thresholds, submitted with the count file referenced (sheets, recounts, investigation notes); the freeze lifted; the segregated cutover stock processed into the new period. Sign-offs: the count lead, the custodian (who is confirming their own custody's verdict — the fairness ritual), and finance above the value line. The whole production, well run, is one long day and a filed bundle; badly run, it is a weekend that generates more errors than it finds — and the difference is entirely this run-book.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Cycle counting: the programme", "13", "<p>The annual count is a storm; the <b>cycle counting programme</b> is weather — small counts, continuously, tuned so every item is verified on a cadence its value deserves. This is the counting regime a serious multi-branch firm actually lives on.</p><p><b>The ABC engine.</b> Stock stratified by value-velocity (the analysis module 7 formalises; the programme consumes it): <b>A items</b> — the vital few holding most of the money — counted frequently (monthly is a working default); <b>B items</b> quarterly; <b>C items</b> — the trivial many — annually, effectively riding the full count. The arithmetic that sells the programme: a few lines counted per day per warehouse, fifteen minutes, covers the entire A-class monthly — the effort lands where the money sits, and the annual count shrinks from a discovery expedition to a formality, because everything valuable was verified eleven times since the last one.</p><p><b>The schedule's craft.</b> Published rhythm, unpublished selection: the team knows counts happen daily; WHICH lines today is not posted in advance (predictable counts are gameable counts — the deterrence job needs the element of surprise), and the selection mixes the scheduled cadence with triggers: items with recent variances (re-verified sooner — a variance is a question about a location, and the follow-up count is the answer's confirmation), items touched by incident (the transit shortfall's item, the return desk's disputed line), negative-stock flags (module 5's alarm — counted the day they appear), and a random seasoning. Identity audits ride the same visits (module 3's serial sightings and batch spot-checks appended to the day's lines — one trip, two verifications).</p><p><b>Mechanics in miniature.</b> Each cycle count is the full count's disciplines at teaspoon scale: blind, counted by someone other than the line's daily handler where the roster allows, material variances recounted, survivors investigated (Chapter 6), adjustments reason-coded (Chapter 7). No freeze is needed for a two-line count — the cutover discipline is simply counting at a quiet moment and posting the reconciliation with an honest timestamp — which is exactly why the cycle programme scales where the full count cannot: it never fights the trading day.</p><p><b>The programme's outputs.</b> Beyond corrected bins: the <b>count accuracy rate</b> (lines counted clean ÷ lines counted — the warehouse's ongoing grade, trended per warehouse and per class), the variance pipeline feeding Chapter 7's shrinkage read with fresh, small, investigable differences instead of an annual heap, and the accountability rhythm — every custodian's areas verified continuously, which is the fairness design running on autopilot. A firm with a healthy cycle programme knows its accuracy this month, its shrinkage this quarter, and faces its auditor with a counting FILE instead of a counting weekend.</p><p><b>Right-sizing.</b> The eleven-person single-site firm runs the same idea smaller: a page of A items counted weekly by rotation, the annual full count, the same blindness and recount rules. The programme is a shape, not a headcount — and the consultant's deliverable is the shape sized to the client: the strata, the cadences, the daily line quota, the trigger list, and the named owner of the schedule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Variance investigation: before you adjust", "14", "<p>A variance that survives recount is a fact: the ledger and the shelf disagree, and the disagreement is real. The amateur move is adjusting it away; the professional move is this chapter — the investigation that makes the adjustment TRUE instead of merely tidy.</p><p><b>Why investigate first.</b> The adjustment writes history (the append-only ledger accepts it forever) and closes the question — and a question closed wrong stays wrong twice: the root cause survives to generate next month's variance, and the adjustment itself may be backwards (the shortage adjusted away at warehouse A that was really a surplus mis-shelved at warehouse B now becomes TWO wrong bins made permanent). The investigation window — variances held open, worked, and only then adjusted — is the discipline; its length is policy (days, not weeks: an aging variance list is its own disease), and its output is a CAUSE attached to every material adjustment.</p><p><b>The taxonomy — where differences actually come from, in rough order of frequency:</b></p><ul><li><b>Unposted or mis-posted documents</b> — the transfer dispatched but never received (the goods sit in transit; the branch counted short against a bin that never received them), the delivery picked but the DN unsubmitted, the receipt keyed to the wrong date. The ledger walk (module 1's diagnostic) finds these in minutes: the item's recent entries against the physical paper trail, and the fix is POSTING the missing truth, not adjusting around it — an adjustment that papers over an unposted document creates a double error the day the document finally posts.</li><li><b>Wrong-warehouse postings</b> — the mirror shortage-and-surplus pair (module 1's disease): warehouse A short exactly what warehouse B is over. The cross-check is cheap (the same item's variances across warehouses, netted) and the fix is the correcting transfer, not two adjustments.</li><li><b>UOM errors</b> — the factor-of-twelve signatures: variances at suspicious ratios (short 110 on a Carton-12 item is one mis-keyed carton row, not 110 missing units). The conversion arithmetic checked before the loss is believed.</li><li><b>Identity drift</b> — quantity right, batches wrong (module 3's orphans): not a stock adjustment at all, but a batch correction with its own discipline.</li><li><b>Count error residue</b> — the recount missed something too; a third look at stubborn variances before the expensive conclusions.</li><li><b>Genuine loss</b> — what remains when the ledger walk, the cross-checks, and the recounts all hold: damage unrecorded, theft, leakage. Now — and only now — the adjustment, reason-coded as loss, feeding the shrinkage read, and (by value and pattern) the escalation the firm's policy names: the custody conversation, the security review, the incident file.</li></ul><p><b>The investigation's own file.</b> Material variances carry their working: what was checked, what was found, who concluded. Thirty lines of notes that turn the adjustment from an assertion into a finding — the difference an auditor, an insurer, or a dismissal hearing will care about intensely. And the aggregate of causes (Chapter 7) is management information of the first order: a warehouse whose variances are 80% unposted documents has a discipline problem with a training fix; one whose variances are unexplained loss has a different problem with a different fix — and only investigation, done every time, can tell the two apart.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Shrinkage: the managed number", "13", "<p>Every investigated variance ends as a reason-coded adjustment, and the codes aggregate into the number this chapter is about: <b>shrinkage</b> — the value of stock the firm lost, stated, trended, and managed like any other cost of trading.</p><p><b>The read.</b> Adjustments-as-loss, aggregated: by period (the trend line), by warehouse (the custody comparison), by category (where the leakage concentrates — the pilferable smalls vs the bulky staples), by cause code (process failures vs damage vs unexplained). Expressed as a RATE — shrinkage value against throughput or holding value — because the raw naira grows with the business and the rate is the honest comparator: quarter on quarter, warehouse against warehouse, the firm against its trade's rough benchmarks. The read exists the moment reason codes are used consistently, which is why Chapter 6 insisted on them: shrinkage is not a special report; it is the investigation discipline's exhaust, collected.</p><p><b>Reading it like management.</b> The rate's LEVEL sets the response scale (a fraction of a percent is the cost of doing business managed well; multiples of it are a program, not a number); the TREND is the alarm (a stable 0.4% is peace; 0.4% becoming 0.9% in two quarters is a change with a cause — new staff? new category? a control that lapsed?); the DISTRIBUTION is the map (one branch at triple the network rate is a located problem — custody, security, or discipline, and the branch comparison is what locates it); and the CAUSE MIX is the prescription: process-heavy shrinkage (unposted, mis-posted, UOM) is fixed by training and the earlier modules' disciplines; damage-heavy by handling and storage; unexplained-heavy by the harder conversations — access control, the counting programme's unpredictability turned up, and where pattern warrants, the investigation the firm's policy and the law provide for. The consultant's framing for owners: you cannot price, insure, or staff against a number you refuse to know — and the firms shocked annually by stocktake write-offs are not unlucky; they are unread.</p><p><b>The deterrence dividend.</b> The read's existence changes the number it reads: custodians who know shrinkage is computed per custody, counts arrive unpredictably, and variances get investigated (not just adjusted) work in a different atmosphere — the honest are protected by the same machinery that exposes the other kind, which is module 1's custodian framing paying its dividend. The atmosphere is the cheapest control in this entire module, and it is built from nothing but consistency: counts that happen, investigations that conclude, and a number that someone visibly reads.</p><p><b>What shrinkage is not.</b> Not a bucket for lazy differences (the uninvestigated variance coded unexplained is a lie with a category), not a write-off holiday (the quarterly shrinkage posting does not replace item-level, reason-coded adjustments — aggregation without granularity manages nothing), and not shameful to report (the owner who wants shrinkage hidden from the monthly pack is asking to be surprised annually instead — the number's job is to be seen small and often). The mature end state: shrinkage on the monthly management page beside consumables and write-offs, boring, small, and watched — the warehouse's cost of reality, priced.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's counting year", "16", "<p>The counting regime, lived for a year. Every chapter clocks in.</p><p><b>The go-live opening (Ch. 3).</b> Cutover weekend: full count first — pairs, blind sheets, batches read from cartons with expiries, the chiller serials sighted. Then the load... and the near-miss that justifies the module: the draft opening reconciliation, keyed by the client's team from their price list <i>because those were the numbers we had</i>, shows Central's stock at ₦61m. The consultant's hour-one check — opening rates against recent purchase invoices — catches it: the rates are SELLING prices. Re-sourced at cost (invoice-level for the A items, documented method for the tail, accountant's sign-off), the honest figure is ₦42m. The ₦19m phantom dies in Draft — the balance sheet never carries it, the margins never lie, and the client's accountant becomes the implementation's loudest advocate. The opening file closes: sheets, sources, sign-offs, documents.</p><p><b>The programme designed (Ch. 5).</b> ABC run on the opening data: 62 A items (71% of value), counted monthly — five lines per day per warehouse, fifteen minutes; B quarterly; C rides the annual. Selection unpublished; triggers wired (variance follow-ups, incident items, negative-stock flags — plus module 3's identity audits riding the visits). The schedule has an owner, and the first month's count accuracy posts at 96.8%.</p><p><b>The investigation (Ch. 6).</b> Month three, a cycle count finds Ibadan short 8 cartons of amoxicillin — survives recount. The investigation window opens: the ledger walk shows a transfer dispatched from Central twelve days prior... and never received — the goods sat in In-Transit the whole time (module 2's aging read had flagged the room that same morning; the two diagnostics converge on one truth). Physical check: the cartons are AT Ibadan — arrived, shelved, receipt never entered by a leave-covering stand-in. The fix is the missing POSTING (the transfer receipt, entered with its honest story), not an adjustment — which would have created the double error the day anyone found the paperwork. Cause coded: unposted document; the stand-in trained; the variance list closes at zero adjustment. The system worked BECAUSE nobody reached for the eraser.</p><p><b>The full count (Ch. 4).</b> Year-end, one Sunday: freeze announced and absolute (trucks rescheduled, tills dark 06:00-14:00), pairs on mapped areas, blind sheets, material variances recounted by fresh pairs. The verified list is short — the cycle programme spent a year keeping it short — and the biggest surviving item is a UOM signature (short exactly 24 on a Carton-12 line: two carton rows keyed as units at a February cycle count, corrected with the conversion checked). Sign-offs: count lead, each custodian, finance. One day, one file, no drama.</p><p><b>The shrinkage read (Ch. 7).</b> Four quarters posted: 0.42%, 0.38%, 0.44%, 0.41% — stable, small, cause-mix dominated by damage and process residue, unexplained under a third of the total and flat. One distribution flag mid-year: Abeokuta's Q2 rate at double the network — investigated (a rear-door access pattern, resolved with a lock policy and two months of trigger counts), back on-trend by Q3. The number lived on the monthly page all year; nobody was surprised in December. Boring — which was the point, and the certificate.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fourth law", "13", "<p>The counting layer's scar tissue. Symptom → disease → fix.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the reconciliation as eraser.</b> Symptom: variances adjusted same-day, uninvestigated; the same lines varying every count. Disease: the question closed without being answered (Ch. 1, 6) — root causes immortal, wrong-way adjustments compounding. Fix: the investigation window as unskippable routine, causes coded on every material adjustment, and the follow-up count that verifies the answer held.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — opening at selling price.</b> Symptom: inflated stock asset; first-quarter margins near zero while trade is brisk. Disease: the at-cost law broken at the one moment it cannot be cheaply unbroken (Ch. 3). Fix: caught in Draft — the hour-one check of opening rates against purchase records; caught late — the supervised revaluation exercise with the accountant, weeks of it, and the lesson institutionalised.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — counting a moving target.</b> Symptom: variances that vanish on recount; count weekends that generate more errors than they find. Disease: the freeze leaked (Ch. 4) — movements mid-count making every sheet a snapshot of a different moment. Fix: the freeze window absolute or the cutover strict; the first investigation question, always: did the freeze hold?</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — effort inverted.</b> Symptom: the trivial tail counted obsessively while A items drift for quarters. Disease: the programme unweighted (Ch. 5) — effort spread by item count instead of value. Fix: the ABC engine, the daily quota landing on the vital few, and the accuracy rate reported per class so the inversion is visible.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — count theatre.</b> Symptom: sheets that match the ledger perfectly, always. Disease: counting from the screen (Ch. 4) — expectations printed, aisles skipped, the blindness rule dead and with it the entire exercise. Fix: blind sheets structurally, pairs and rotation, spot re-counts of counted areas by the schedule owner — and the accuracy rate treated with suspicion when it is TOO perfect.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the unread number.</b> Symptom: adjustments posted for years; nobody can state the shrinkage rate. Disease: the exhaust never collected (Ch. 7) — reason codes inconsistent or absent, the annual shock arriving on schedule. Fix: the code list short and enforced, the quarterly read on the management page, and the trend owned by a name.</p><p><b>The fourth law.</b> Module 1: the tree is the map of custody. Module 2: stock moves only by documents. Module 3: identity is operational. This module: <b>the count is a dialogue between the ledger and the shelf — investigate before you adjust, and adjust in the open.</b> Open at cost, count blind, freeze honestly, recount before believing, name every cause, and read the number the causes add up to. A firm that keeps this law faces auditors with files, custodians with fairness, and December with boredom — and its ledger, tested continuously and found truthful, becomes the asset the next module prices: valuation, where every quantity this module verified meets the money it represents.</p>"),
]

